// MLRift Standard Library — int8 quantized neural-network kernels
// ===========================================================================
//
// A TFLite-Micro-compatible int8 inference kernel set written in plain MLRift.
// It compiles and runs identically on x86_64 (host), riscv32 and xtensa.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why every line of this file is integer-only and 32-bit-only
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// riscv32 and xtensa reject `u64`/`i64` and every float type at sema
// (src/analysis.mlr). There is therefore:
//
//   * NO 64-bit integer anywhere. The 64-bit intermediate that int8
//     requantization fundamentally needs (a 32x32 product) is carried as an
//     explicit (hi, lo) pair of u32 and built from 16-bit half-products —
//     see nn_umulhi32. The xtensa backend emits only MULL (no MULUH/MULSH),
//     so a hardware multiply-high is not available even where the ISA has one.
//
//   * NO floats. Quantization scales are (int32 multiplier, int32 shift)
//     fixed-point pairs exactly as TFLite stores them. Producing that pair
//     from a float scale is an offline/host job; on-device we only consume it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Host / MCU bit-exactness — the one rule that makes it work
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// On x86_64 every MLRift variable lives in a 64-bit slot, so `u32` arithmetic
// does NOT wrap at 32 bits; on riscv32/xtensa it does. Any expression that can
// exceed 32 bits is therefore masked with `& 0xFFFFFFFF` explicitly. The mask
// is a no-op on the 32-bit targets and forces the host to agree with them.
// Signed values are converted through nn_u32_to_i32 / nn_i32_to_u32 rather
// than being reinterpreted implicitly. Verified: x86_64, xtensa (qemu lx60)
// and riscv32 (qemu virt) all produce byte-identical output, and that output
// is exactly equal to an independent Python reference written from the
// gemmlowp/TFLite definitions with native 64-bit products (tests/nn/).
//
// Compiler gotcha, learned the hard way: an expression whose operands are ALL
// literals is const-folded in 64 bits, and a folded value above 0xFFFFFFFF is
// rejected by the xtensa emitter ("64-bit constant not supported"). Never
// write e.g. `shr(0 - 9, 1)` with two literals; keep one operand dynamic.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pointers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MLRift has no pointer type; addresses are plain integers. Every buffer here
// is a `u32` address plus explicit dimensions.
//
// On the 32-bit targets that is the native width. ON THE HOST, a u32 address
// means tensors MUST live in `static` arrays: MLRift places statics in the
// data section around 0x400000, which fits in 32 bits, whereas the stack
// (~0x7fff_ffff_xxxx) and alloc()'s mmap results do not. This is also the
// right thing on an MCU, where all tensors are static .bss anyway.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tensor memory layout (stated, because it is a choice)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Batch is always 1. All layouts match TFLite's.
//
//   activations       int8   NHWC : [H][W][C]
//                            index = ((h * W) + w) * C + c
//
//   conv2d weights    int8   OHWI : [out_c][kh][kw][in_c]
//                            index = (((oc * kh) + i) * kw + j) * in_c + ic
//
//   depthwise weights int8   1HWC : [1][kh][kw][out_c],
//                            out_c = in_c * depth_multiplier
//                            index = ((i * kw) + j) * out_c + oc
//                            oc    = ic * depth_multiplier + m
//
//   gemv / gemm B     int8   row-major [N][K] (i.e. already transposed:
//                            each output n owns a contiguous K-vector)
//
//   bias              int32  [out_c]                (little-endian words)
//   multiplier        int32  [out_c]  or  [1]
//   shift             int32  [out_c]  or  [1]
//
// `q_stride` selects between per-channel and per-tensor quantization:
//   q_stride = 1  ->  multiplier[oc], shift[oc]   (per channel)
//   q_stride = 0  ->  multiplier[0],  shift[0]    (per tensor)
//
// Output spatial extent is NOT a parameter of the spatial kernels; it is
// always nn_out_dim(in, k, stride, pad) — for both convolutions and both
// poolings. Two reasons: it removes a whole class of caller bug (a mismatched
// out_h silently walks off the end of the output tensor), and the xtensa
// backend allows at most 22 call arguments (6 registers + 16 overflow slots),
// which a fully explicit conv2d signature exceeds. Call nn_out_dim yourself
// to size the output buffer. Padding is symmetric (`pad` on both sides); the
// asymmetric half of TFLite's SAME padding is not modelled.
//
// Padding is implicit-zero in the REAL domain, which in the quantized domain
// means the padded input value equals in_zp, so (in_val - in_zp) * w == 0.
// The kernels therefore simply skip out-of-bounds taps — that is exact, not an
// approximation.
//
// A bias pointer of 0 means "no bias".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Numerics
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Requantization follows gemmlowp / TFLite exactly:
//
//   MultiplyByQuantizedMultiplier(x, m, s)
//     = RoundingDivideByPOT(
//           SaturatingRoundingDoublingHighMul(x << max(s,0), m),
//           max(-s, 0))
//
// SaturatingRoundingDoublingHighMul computes (a*b + nudge) / 2^31 over a true
// 64-bit product with C truncation-toward-zero, saturating the single
// INT32_MIN * INT32_MIN case to INT32_MAX. RoundingDivideByPOT rounds half
// AWAY FROM ZERO (its negative-value threshold bump is what makes -1.5 go to
// -2 rather than -1). Both are reproduced bit-for-bit below.
// ===========================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 1 — 32-bit fixed-point core
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn nn_i32_max() -> i32 {
    i32 v = 2147483647
    return v
}

fn nn_i32_min() -> i32 {
    u32 b = 0x80000000
    return nn_u32_to_i32(b)
}

// Reinterpret a 32-bit pattern as a signed 32-bit integer.
// On the 32-bit targets this is the identity on the bits; on the host it is
// the sign extension the hardware would have done for free.
fn nn_u32_to_i32(u32 v) -> i32 {
    u32 x = v & 0xFFFFFFFF
    if (x & 0x80000000) == 0 {
        i32 p = x
        return p
    }
    // magnitude = (~x) + 1; for x == 0x80000000 this is 0x80000000 and the
    // negation below lands exactly on INT32_MIN on both target classes.
    u32 mag = (x ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1
    i32 r = mag
    return 0 - r
}

// Reinterpret a signed 32-bit integer as its 32-bit pattern.
fn nn_i32_to_u32(i32 v) -> u32 {
    u32 p = v
    return p & 0xFFFFFFFF
}

// |v| as an unsigned 32-bit magnitude. |INT32_MIN| == 0x80000000 is exact.
fn nn_abs_u32(i32 v) -> u32 {
    if v < 0 {
        i32 n = 0 - v
        return nn_i32_to_u32(n)
    }
    return nn_i32_to_u32(v)
}

// High 32 bits of a 32x32 unsigned product, from four 16-bit half-products.
// No intermediate here can exceed 32 bits, so no masking is needed and the
// host agrees with the 32-bit targets for free (0xFFFFFFFF^2 included).
fn nn_umulhi32(u32 a, u32 b) -> u32 {
    u32 x = a & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u32 y = b & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u32 al = x & 0xFFFF
    u32 ah = x >> 16
    u32 bl = y & 0xFFFF
    u32 bh = y >> 16
    u32 lo = al * bl
    u32 m1 = ah * bl
    u32 m2 = al * bh
    u32 t = (lo >> 16) + (m1 & 0xFFFF) + (m2 & 0xFFFF)
    return ah * bh + (m1 >> 16) + (m2 >> 16) + (t >> 16)
}

// Low 32 bits of a 32x32 unsigned product.
fn nn_umullo32(u32 a, u32 b) -> u32 {
    u32 x = a & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u32 y = b & 0xFFFFFFFF
    return (x * y) & 0xFFFFFFFF
}

// High 32 bits of a 32x32 SIGNED product.
// Computed from the magnitude product and then negated as a 64-bit (hi, lo)
// pair. The usual `umulhi - (a<0 ? b : 0) - (b<0 ? a : 0)` correction is
// deliberately avoided: it relies on 32-bit wrap-around, which the host
// does not do.
fn nn_mulhi_i32(i32 a, i32 b) -> i32 {
    u32 ua = nn_abs_u32(a)
    u32 ub = nn_abs_u32(b)
    u32 hi = nn_umulhi32(ua, ub)
    u32 lo = nn_umullo32(ua, ub)

    u32 neg = 0
    if a < 0 { neg = 1 }
    if b < 0 { neg = neg ^ 1 }
    if hi == 0 {
        if lo == 0 { neg = 0 }
    }
    if neg == 1 {
        u32 carry = 0
        if lo == 0 { carry = 1 }
        u32 nlo = ((lo ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        u32 nhi = ((hi ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + carry) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        lo = nlo
        hi = nhi
    }
    return nn_u32_to_i32(hi)
}

// gemmlowp SaturatingRoundingDoublingHighMul:
//   nudge   = (a*b >= 0) ? 2^30 : 1 - 2^30
//   result  = (int32)((a*b + nudge) / 2^31)     // C truncation toward zero
//   INT32_MIN * INT32_MIN saturates to INT32_MAX
fn nn_srdhm(i32 a, i32 b) -> i32 {
    u32 ua = nn_abs_u32(a)
    u32 ub = nn_abs_u32(b)

    // |a| == 2^31 only for INT32_MIN, so this is exactly the overflow case.
    if ua == 0x80000000 {
        if ub == 0x80000000 { return nn_i32_max() }
    }

    u32 hi = nn_umulhi32(ua, ub)
    u32 lo = nn_umullo32(ua, ub)

    u32 neg = 0
    if a < 0 { neg = 1 }
    if b < 0 { neg = neg ^ 1 }
    if hi == 0 {
        if lo == 0 { neg = 0 }
    }
    if neg == 1 {
        u32 carry = 0
        if lo == 0 { carry = 1 }
        u32 nlo = ((lo ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        u32 nhi = ((hi ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + carry) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        lo = nlo
        hi = nhi
    }

    // nudge as a sign-extended 64-bit addend:
    //   +2^30           -> (0x00000000, 0x40000000)
    //   1 - 2^30        -> (0xFFFFFFFF, 0xC0000001)
    u32 add_hi = 0
    u32 add_lo = 0x40000000
    if neg == 1 {
        add_hi = 0xFFFFFFFF
        add_lo = 0xC0000001
    }
    u32 slo = (lo + add_lo) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    u32 c2 = 0
    if slo < lo { c2 = 1 }
    u32 shi = (hi + add_hi + c2) & 0xFFFFFFFF

    // (hi, lo) >> 31, keeping bits 62..31 — this is the FLOOR of the division.
    u32 q = ((shi << 1) | (slo >> 31)) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    i32 res = nn_u32_to_i32(q)

    // C divides toward zero, so a negative value with a non-zero remainder
    // must be bumped from floor to ceil.
    if (shi & 0x80000000) != 0 {
        if (slo & 0x7FFFFFFF) != 0 { res = res + 1 }
    }
    return res
}

// gemmlowp RoundingDivideByPOT — divide by 2^exponent rounding half AWAY
// FROM ZERO. The `+1` on the threshold for negative x is what distinguishes
// this from round-half-up and is required for TFLite equivalence.
fn nn_rdbpot(i32 x, u32 exponent) -> i32 {
    u32 e = exponent
    if e == 0 { return x }
    if e > 31 { e = 31 }
    i32 mask = (1 << e) - 1
    i32 remainder = x & mask
    i32 threshold = mask >> 1
    if x < 0 { threshold = threshold + 1 }
    i32 r = x >> e
    if remainder > threshold { r = r + 1 }
    return r
}

// TFLite MultiplyByQuantizedMultiplier. `shift` is TFLite's signed shift:
// positive means shift the input LEFT first, negative means divide the
// product right afterwards. Valid range is [-31, 31].
fn nn_mul_by_qm(i32 x, i32 multiplier, i32 shift) -> i32 {
    u32 left_shift = 0
    u32 right_shift = 0
    if shift > 0 {
        left_shift = shift
    } else {
        i32 ns = 0 - shift
        right_shift = ns
    }
    if left_shift > 31 { left_shift = 31 }

    i32 xs = x
    if left_shift > 0 {
        u32 ux = nn_i32_to_u32(x)
        ux = (ux << left_shift) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        xs = nn_u32_to_i32(ux)
    }
    i32 h = nn_srdhm(xs, multiplier)
    return nn_rdbpot(h, right_shift)
}

fn nn_clamp_i32(i32 v, i32 lo, i32 hi) -> i32 {
    if v < lo { return lo }
    if v > hi { return hi }
    return v
}

// Requantize an int32 accumulator to int8 with no output zero point and the
// full int8 range. Returned as i32 in [-128, 127] (MLRift has no i8 return
// slot distinct from i32; store it with nn_store_i8).
fn nn_requantize(i32 acc, i32 multiplier, i32 shift) -> i32 {
    i32 v = nn_mul_by_qm(acc, multiplier, shift)
    i32 lo = 0 - 128
    return nn_clamp_i32(v, lo, 127)
}

// Full form: adds the output zero point and clamps to an activation range
// (act_min/act_max are already in the quantized domain, as TFLite stores
// them — a fused ReLU is just act_min = out_zp).
//
// THIS IS THE READABLE DEFINITION AND IT IS NOT WHAT THE KERNELS CALL.
// nn_requantize_zp() below is the same function with every call inlined; this
// one survives as the reference the equivalence test checks it against, so the
// composition of nn_mul_by_qm / nn_srdhm / nn_rdbpot stays the spec.
fn nn_requantize_zp_ref(i32 acc, i32 multiplier, i32 shift, i32 out_zp, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) -> i32 {
    i32 v = nn_mul_by_qm(acc, multiplier, shift) + out_zp
    return nn_clamp_i32(v, act_min, act_max)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The requantization hot path, flattened
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every convolution output goes through exactly one of these, and on real
// ESP32 silicon the composed version above measured ~2600 CPU cycles per
// output — about half of a 20 s YuNet@64 inference, for what is arithmetically
// a multiply and a shift. The cost is structural, not arithmetic: the composed
// form is six functions deep (nn_mul_by_qm -> nn_srdhm -> nn_abs_u32 twice,
// nn_umulhi32, nn_umullo32, nn_u32_to_i32; then nn_rdbpot; then
// nn_clamp_i32), each one a real call0, and the register allocator has only
// IR_NUM_REGS = 6 colours, so nearly every intermediate spills to the frame
// and is reloaded across each call.
//
// What follows is the SAME function with all of that inlined into one frame.
// It is not a fast path, an approximation or a special case: it must return
// exactly nn_requantize_zp_ref(...) for every input in the declared domain,
// and nn_requantize_selftest() below proves that on every target the kernels
// run on (host 64-bit slots, xtensa and riscv32 native 32-bit wrap).
//
// The one non-obvious rewrite is the (sign, magnitude) extraction. The
// composed form does nn_abs_u32(nn_u32_to_i32(ux)), which round-trips through
// a signed value; here the magnitude is taken straight off the bit pattern as
//   (ux & 0x80000000) ? ((~ux) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF : ux
// which agrees on the whole range including ux == 0x80000000, where both give
// 0x80000000 (|INT32_MIN| is exactly representable as an unsigned magnitude —
// that is the same identity nn_abs_u32's comment relies on).
//
// Everything else is a line-for-line inline, deliberately keeping the
// composed version's masking so the host and the 32-bit targets agree for the
// same reasons they already did.
fn nn_requantize_zp(i32 acc, i32 multiplier, i32 shift, i32 out_zp, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) -> i32 {
    // --- nn_mul_by_qm: split TFLite's signed shift into the two halves ---
    u32 left_shift = 0
    u32 right_shift = 0
    if shift > 0 {
        left_shift = nn_i32_to_u32(shift)
        if left_shift > 31 { left_shift = 31 }
    } else {
        i32 ns = 0 - shift
        right_shift = nn_i32_to_u32(ns)
    }

    // xs = acc << left_shift, carried as its 32-bit pattern.
    //
    // ⚠️ nn_i32_to_u32() HERE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION. Writing the
    // equivalent-looking `u32 ux = acc  ux = ux & 0xFFFFFFFF` makes mlrc treat
    // ux — and everything derived from it — as SIGNED on the 32-bit backends:
    // further down, `slo < lo` becomes a signed compare and `slo >> 31` becomes
    // an arithmetic shift, so 0x80000000 >> 31 yields 0xFFFFFFFF instead of 1.
    // The host, where u32 lives in a 64-bit slot and 0x80000000 is genuinely
    // positive, gets the right answer either way — so this is invisible until
    // it is run on xtensa or riscv32, where it is catastrophic (12856 of 14742
    // requantizations wrong). Passing through a fn DECLARED to return u32
    // restores unsigned provenance. This is the implicit-reinterpretation trap
    // the file header warns about; it applies to every i32 -> u32 assignment
    // whose result is later compared or right-shifted.
    u32 ux = nn_i32_to_u32(acc)
    if left_shift > 0 { ux = (ux << left_shift) & 0xFFFFFFFF }

    // --- nn_srdhm(xs, multiplier), inlined ---
    // sign and magnitude of the i32 whose pattern is ux
    u32 neg = 0
    u32 ua = ux
    if (ux & 0x80000000) != 0 {
        neg = 1
        ua = ((ux ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    }
    // sign and magnitude of the multiplier (nn_i32_to_u32 for the same
    // unsigned-provenance reason as ux above — ub feeds the same compares)
    u32 ub = nn_i32_to_u32(multiplier)
    if multiplier < 0 {
        neg = neg ^ 1
        ub = ((ub ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    }

    i32 h = 0
    u32 sat = 0
    // |a| == 2^31 only for INT32_MIN, so this is exactly gemmlowp's single
    // overflow case. Note it saturates the DOUBLING-HIGH-MUL result, so the
    // right shift below still applies — an early return here would be wrong.
    if ua == 0x80000000 {
        if ub == 0x80000000 { sat = 1 }
    }
    if sat != 0 {
        h = nn_i32_max()
    } else {
        // nn_umulhi32 from four 16-bit half-products; no intermediate here can
        // exceed 32 bits, so no masking is needed and the host agrees for free.
        u32 al = ua & 0xFFFF
        u32 ah = ua >> 16
        u32 bl = ub & 0xFFFF
        u32 bh = ub >> 16
        u32 pll = al * bl
        u32 m1 = ah * bl
        u32 m2 = al * bh
        u32 t = (pll >> 16) + (m1 & 0xFFFF) + (m2 & 0xFFFF)
        u32 hi = ah * bh + (m1 >> 16) + (m2 >> 16) + (t >> 16)
        // nn_umullo32
        u32 lo = (ua * ub) & 0xFFFFFFFF

        if hi == 0 {
            if lo == 0 { neg = 0 }
        }
        if neg == 1 {
            u32 carry = 0
            if lo == 0 { carry = 1 }
            u32 nlo = ((lo ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
            u32 nhi = ((hi ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + carry) & 0xFFFFFFFF
            lo = nlo
            hi = nhi
        }

        // nudge as a sign-extended 64-bit addend:
        //   +2^30    -> (0x00000000, 0x40000000)
        //   1 - 2^30 -> (0xFFFFFFFF, 0xC0000001)
        u32 add_hi = 0
        u32 add_lo = 0x40000000
        if neg == 1 {
            add_hi = 0xFFFFFFFF
            add_lo = 0xC0000001
        }
        u32 slo = (lo + add_lo) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        u32 c2 = 0
        if slo < lo { c2 = 1 }
        u32 shi = (hi + add_hi + c2) & 0xFFFFFFFF

        // (hi, lo) >> 31 keeping bits 62..31 — the FLOOR of the division.
        u32 q = ((shi << 1) | (slo >> 31)) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        // nn_u32_to_i32(q)
        if (q & 0x80000000) == 0 {
            h = q
        } else {
            u32 mag = ((q ^ 0xFFFFFFFF) + 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF
            i32 hr = mag
            h = 0 - hr
        }
        // C divides toward zero, so a negative value with a non-zero remainder
        // must be bumped from floor to ceil.
        if (shi & 0x80000000) != 0 {
            if (slo & 0x7FFFFFFF) != 0 { h = h + 1 }
        }
    }

    // --- nn_rdbpot(h, right_shift), inlined ---
    i32 res = h
    if right_shift != 0 {
        u32 e = right_shift
        if e > 31 { e = 31 }
        i32 mask = (1 << e) - 1
        i32 remainder = h & mask
        i32 threshold = mask >> 1
        if h < 0 { threshold = threshold + 1 }
        res = h >> e
        if remainder > threshold { res = res + 1 }
    }

    // --- + out_zp, then nn_clamp_i32 ---
    i32 v = res + out_zp
    if v < act_min { return act_min }
    if v > act_max { return act_max }
    return v
}

// Prove nn_requantize_zp == nn_requantize_zp_ref. Returns the number of
// disagreements, so 0 is a pass; the caller prints it.
//
// The grid is chosen for the places the two forms could plausibly diverge
// rather than for size: the accumulator values are the int32 extremes and the
// bit patterns that straddle the sign boundary and the rounding thresholds,
// the multipliers include both INT32_MIN and INT32_MAX (the saturating
// INT32_MIN*INT32_MIN case) plus the [2^30, 2^31) band a real quantizer
// actually emits, and `shift` sweeps the entire declared [-31, 31] including
// the left-shift half that YuNet never exercises. The zero-point and clamp
// arguments are varied too so the tail is covered. An LCG then adds pseudo
// random triples over the full 32-bit range on top.
//
// It runs on host, xtensa and riscv32, which matters: the host carries u32 in
// 64-bit slots and does NOT wrap, so an inlining mistake that only shows up
// under real 32-bit truncation would be invisible if this were host-only.
// Test vectors as 32-bit BIT PATTERNS, not as i32. A `static i32[]` element
// load does not sign-extend on the host (it yields the raw 4 bytes in a 64-bit
// slot, so -1 comes back as 4294967295 — a value outside the i32 domain that
// both functions are then free to disagree about). Storing patterns and
// converting with nn_u32_to_i32 on the way out is the same discipline
// nn_load_i32 already uses, and it makes the host and the 32-bit targets feed
// the two implementations identical arguments.
static u32[13] nn_rq_accs
static u32[9] nn_rq_mults

fn nn_requantize_selftest() -> u32 {
    u32 bad = 0

    nn_rq_accs[0] = 0
    nn_rq_accs[1] = 1
    nn_rq_accs[2] = 0xFFFFFFFF          // -1
    nn_rq_accs[3] = 2
    nn_rq_accs[4] = 0xFFFFFFFE          // -2
    nn_rq_accs[5] = 127
    nn_rq_accs[6] = 0xFFFFFF80          // -128
    nn_rq_accs[7] = 65536
    nn_rq_accs[8] = 0xFFFF0000          // -65536
    nn_rq_accs[9] = 0x40000000          // 2^30
    nn_rq_accs[10] = 0xC0000000         // -2^30
    nn_rq_accs[11] = 0x7FFFFFFF         // INT32_MAX
    nn_rq_accs[12] = 0x80000000         // INT32_MIN

    nn_rq_mults[0] = 1
    nn_rq_mults[1] = 0xFFFFFFFF         // -1
    nn_rq_mults[2] = 0x40000000         // 2^30
    nn_rq_mults[3] = 0x40000001
    nn_rq_mults[4] = 1500000000
    nn_rq_mults[5] = 0x7FFFFFFF         // INT32_MAX
    nn_rq_mults[6] = 0xC0000000         // -2^30
    nn_rq_mults[7] = 0x80000000         // INT32_MIN
    nn_rq_mults[8] = 0

    u32 ai = 0
    while ai < 13 {
        u32 mi = 0
        while mi < 9 {
            i32 av = nn_u32_to_i32(nn_rq_accs[ai])
            i32 mv = nn_u32_to_i32(nn_rq_mults[mi])
            i32 sh = 0 - 31
            while sh <= 31 {
                i32 got = nn_requantize_zp(av, mv, sh, 0, 0 - 128, 127)
                i32 wnt = nn_requantize_zp_ref(av, mv, sh, 0, 0 - 128, 127)
                if got != wnt { bad = bad + 1 }
                // again with a non-zero zero point and an asymmetric clamp
                got = nn_requantize_zp(av, mv, sh, 7, 0 - 20, 100)
                wnt = nn_requantize_zp_ref(av, mv, sh, 7, 0 - 20, 100)
                if got != wnt { bad = bad + 1 }
                sh = sh + 1
            }
            mi = mi + 1
        }
        ai = ai + 1
    }

    // Pseudo-random sweep. The LCG is the same one tests/nn uses; 20000
    // triples is a few million equivalent comparisons after the grid above
    // and still finishes fast enough for qemu.
    u32 s = 987654321
    u32 k = 0
    while k < 20000 {
        s = ((s * 1103515245) + 12345) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        i32 a = nn_u32_to_i32(s)
        s = ((s * 1103515245) + 12345) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        i32 m = nn_u32_to_i32(s)
        s = ((s * 1103515245) + 12345) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        u32 shu = (s >> 13) % 63
        i32 sh2 = shu
        sh2 = sh2 - 31
        s = ((s * 1103515245) + 12345) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        u32 zu = (s >> 17) % 255
        i32 zp = zu
        zp = zp - 128
        i32 g = nn_requantize_zp(a, m, sh2, zp, 0 - 128, 127)
        i32 w = nn_requantize_zp_ref(a, m, sh2, zp, 0 - 128, 127)
        if g != w { bad = bad + 1 }
        k = k + 1
    }
    return bad
}

// Signed divide truncating toward zero (C semantics), implemented on
// magnitudes so it behaves identically on every backend.
fn nn_sdiv_trunc(i32 a, u32 b) -> i32 {
    if b == 0 { return 0 }
    if a >= 0 {
        u32 ua = a
        u32 q = ua / b
        i32 r = q
        return r
    }
    u32 um = nn_abs_u32(a)
    u32 qq = um / b
    i32 rr = qq
    return 0 - rr
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 2 — typed memory access
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Load one int8 element, sign-extended into an i32.
fn nn_load_i8(u32 p) -> i32 {
    u32 b = load8(p)
    i32 v = b & 0xFF
    if v > 127 { return v - 256 }
    return v
}

// Store the low byte of a (already clamped) i32 as int8.
fn nn_store_i8(u32 p, i32 v) {
    u32 u = nn_i32_to_u32(v)
    store8(p, u & 0xFF)
}

fn nn_load_i32(u32 p) -> i32 {
    u32 w = load32(p)
    return nn_u32_to_i32(w)
}

fn nn_store_i32(u32 p, i32 v) {
    store32(p, nn_i32_to_u32(v))
}

// Output spatial extent for a conv/pool with symmetric padding.
fn nn_out_dim(u32 in_dim, u32 k, u32 stride, u32 pad) -> u32 {
    u32 eff = (in_dim + (2 * pad))
    if eff < k { return 0 }
    return ((eff - k) / stride) + 1
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Valid tap range — the padding test, hoisted out of the inner loops
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A kernel index k contributes to output position `out_pos` exactly when
//
//     0 <= out_pos * stride + k - pad < in_dim
//
// which is the pair of bounds checks the spatial kernels used to evaluate PER
// TAP. Solving it for k gives the half-open range
//
//     k in [ max(0, pad - out_pos*stride),  min(k_n, in_dim + pad - out_pos*stride) )
//
// so the checks become two computations per output ROW/COLUMN instead of two
// branches per multiply-accumulate. This is a pure hoist, not a change of
// behaviour: the range contains exactly the taps the per-tap test admitted, in
// the same increasing order, so every accumulator sees the same addends in the
// same sequence and the result is bit-identical. (Order matters here only in
// principle — these are exact integer adds — but keeping it makes the
// equivalence trivial to see.)
//
// Both return values are clamped into [0, k_n], and the caller must treat
// hi <= lo as "no valid taps" (which is reachable: a window can fall entirely
// in the padding).
fn nn_tap_lo(u32 out_pos, u32 stride, u32 pad, u32 k_n) -> u32 {
    u32 base = out_pos * stride
    if pad <= base { return 0 }
    u32 d = pad - base
    if d > k_n { return k_n }
    return d
}

fn nn_tap_hi(u32 out_pos, u32 stride, u32 pad, u32 k_n, u32 in_dim) -> u32 {
    u32 base = out_pos * stride
    u32 lim = in_dim + pad
    if base >= lim { return 0 }
    u32 h = lim - base
    if h > k_n { return k_n }
    return h
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 2b — int8 dot-product inner loops
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// These two functions exist for ONE reason, and it is not tidiness: mlrc
// allocates registers per FUNCTION, by graph colouring, with IR_NUM_REGS = 6
// colours. nn_conv2d_int8 and nn_depthwise_conv2d_int8 carry roughly 25 locals
// that are live around their innermost loop (extents, strides, tap ranges,
// base addresses, the requantization parameters, the loop indices at four
// levels), so the innermost loop's own values lose the colouring and every
// single multiply-accumulate turns into a run of spill loads and stores
// against the frame. That is measurable: with the loop inlined in the caller a
// MAC cost ~125 CPU cycles on the ESP32, for what is two byte loads, a compare
// each, a MULL and an add.
//
// Pulled out here, the four values that matter — the accumulator, the two
// pointers and the counter — fit the budget, and the call is paid once per
// kernel row instead of once per element.
//
// Both are unrolled 4x with a remainder tail. The unrolled body deliberately
// REUSES one `a`/`b` pair rather than naming four, so the peak live count
// stays at the same four-plus-two and the unroll does not spend the registers
// it is trying to save. Taps are still visited in strictly ascending order, so
// the integer accumulation is bit-identical to the rolled form.
//
// The sign extension is nn_load_i8's body MINUS its `& 0xFF`. That mask is
// provably dead: every backend's byte load already zero-extends (xtensa l8ui,
// riscv32 lbu, x86_64 movzx), so load8 can only return 0..255. Dropping it is
// not cosmetic under this codegen — materializing the constant 0xFF costs a
// movi, a spill store, two spill reloads, the AND and another spill store, i.e.
// ~6 instructions per operand, twice per multiply-accumulate. Measured on
// silicon: 542.4M -> 486.5M cycles, 1.115x, for deleting 20 dead masks.
// `iv - in_zp` is the same subtraction the naive kernels did.
//
// TRIED AND REJECTED: fusing nn_dot_i8_str's caller-side kernel-ROW loop into
// it, so a whole depthwise window was one call instead of one per row. It made
// depthwise 1.1% SLOWER (149.9M -> 151.6M cycles). Ten parameters puts four of
// them in the caller's overflow slots and pushes the callee to 16 locals, and
// that costs more than the two call0s it removes. The per-row call stays.

// Unit stride on both operands — conv2d and pointwise, where a whole kernel
// row's (kj, ic) taps are contiguous in both NHWC activations and OHWI weights.
fn nn_dot_i8_run(i32 acc, u32 p, u32 q, u32 n, i32 in_zp) -> i32 {
    i32 s = acc
    u32 ip = p
    u32 wp = q
    u32 k = n
    while k >= 4 {
        i32 a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        i32 b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        a = load8(ip + 1)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp + 1)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        a = load8(ip + 2)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp + 2)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        a = load8(ip + 3)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp + 3)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + 4
        wp = wp + 4
        k = k - 4
    }
    while k > 0 {
        i32 a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        i32 b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + 1
        wp = wp + 1
        k = k - 1
    }
    return s
}

// Arbitrary per-tap strides — depthwise, where one output channel's taps sit
// in_c apart in the activations and out_c apart in the 1HWC weights.
fn nn_dot_i8_str(i32 acc, u32 p, u32 q, u32 n, u32 ps, u32 qs, i32 in_zp) -> i32 {
    i32 s = acc
    u32 ip = p
    u32 wp = q
    u32 k = n
    while k >= 4 {
        i32 a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        i32 b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + ps
        wp = wp + qs
        a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + ps
        wp = wp + qs
        a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + ps
        wp = wp + qs
        a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + ps
        wp = wp + qs
        k = k - 4
    }
    while k > 0 {
        i32 a = load8(ip)
        if a > 127 { a = a - 256 }
        i32 b = load8(wp)
        if b > 127 { b = b - 256 }
        s = s + ((a - in_zp) * b)
        ip = ip + ps
        wp = wp + qs
        k = k - 1
    }
    return s
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 3 — convolution kernels
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// General int8 convolution. NHWC activations, OHWI weights, int32 bias,
// per-channel (q_stride = 1) or per-tensor (q_stride = 0) requantization.
fn nn_conv2d_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c, i32 in_zp,
                  u32 w_p, u32 kh, u32 kw,
                  u32 bias_p, u32 mult_p, u32 shift_p, u32 q_stride,
                  u32 out_p, u32 out_c, i32 out_zp,
                  u32 stride_h, u32 stride_w, u32 pad_h, u32 pad_w,
                  i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 out_h = nn_out_dim(in_h, kh, stride_h, pad_h)
    u32 out_w = nn_out_dim(in_w, kw, stride_w, pad_w)
    // (The signed ih_i/iw_i/pad_*_i copies the naive form needed for its
    // per-tap bounds tests are gone: nn_tap_lo/nn_tap_hi do that in u32.)

    // Row strides, computed once. `k_row` is the weight bytes one kernel row
    // spans and `k_oc` the bytes one output channel's whole kernel spans, so
    // every weight address below is an add rather than the four-deep multiply
    // chain ((((oc*kh)+ki)*kw)+kj)*in_c the naive form recomputed per element.
    u32 in_row_bytes = in_w * in_c
    u32 k_row = kw * in_c
    u32 k_oc = kh * k_row
    u32 op_run = out_p                 // the output is written strictly in order

    u32 oy = 0
    while oy < out_h {
        // Valid kernel-row range for this output row (see nn_tap_lo/hi).
        u32 ki_lo = nn_tap_lo(oy, stride_h, pad_h, kh)
        u32 ki_hi = nn_tap_hi(oy, stride_h, pad_h, kh, in_h)
        if ki_hi < ki_lo { ki_hi = ki_lo }
        u32 iy0 = 0
        if ki_hi > ki_lo { iy0 = ((oy * stride_h) + ki_lo) - pad_h }

        u32 ox = 0
        while ox < out_w {
            u32 kj_lo = nn_tap_lo(ox, stride_w, pad_w, kw)
            u32 kj_hi = nn_tap_hi(ox, stride_w, pad_w, kw, in_w)
            if kj_hi < kj_lo { kj_hi = kj_lo }
            u32 ix0 = 0
            if kj_hi > kj_lo { ix0 = ((ox * stride_w) + kj_lo) - pad_w }

            // ONE CONTIGUOUS RUN PER KERNEL ROW. Within a kernel row the taps
            // kj_lo..kj_hi-1 map to ADJACENT input columns (kj advances the
            // input x by exactly 1 whatever stride_w is — stride only moves the
            // base), and NHWC puts adjacent columns' channel vectors back to
            // back. The OHWI weights are contiguous over (kj, ic) for a fixed
            // (oc, ki) for the same reason. So the kj loop and the ic loop
            // collapse into a single flat run of `run` bytes on both operands,
            // visiting exactly the same (kj, ic) pairs in exactly the same
            // order as the nested form did.
            u32 run = (kj_hi - kj_lo) * in_c
            u32 in_base = in_p + (((iy0 * in_w) + ix0) * in_c)
            u32 w_base = w_p + (ki_lo * k_row) + (kj_lo * in_c)

            u32 oc = 0
            while oc < out_c {
                i32 acc = 0
                if bias_p != 0 { acc = nn_load_i32(bias_p + (oc * 4)) }

                u32 ip = in_base
                u32 wp = w_base + (oc * k_oc)
                u32 ki = ki_lo
                while ki < ki_hi {
                    acc = nn_dot_i8_run(acc, ip, wp, run, in_zp)
                    ip = ip + in_row_bytes
                    wp = wp + k_row
                    ki = ki + 1
                }

                u32 qi = (oc * q_stride) * 4
                i32 m = nn_load_i32(mult_p + qi)
                i32 sh = nn_load_i32(shift_p + qi)
                i32 ov = nn_requantize_zp(acc, m, sh, out_zp, act_min, act_max)
                nn_store_i8(op_run, ov)
                op_run = op_run + 1
                oc = oc + 1
            }
            ox = ox + 1
        }
        oy = oy + 1
    }
}

// Depthwise convolution — the workhorse of MobileNet-class detectors.
// Weights are [1][kh][kw][out_c] with out_c = in_c * depth_mult; output
// channel oc = ic * depth_mult + m draws from input channel ic.
fn nn_depthwise_conv2d_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c, i32 in_zp,
                            u32 w_p, u32 kh, u32 kw,
                            u32 bias_p, u32 mult_p, u32 shift_p, u32 q_stride,
                            u32 out_p, i32 out_zp,
                            u32 stride_h, u32 stride_w, u32 pad_h, u32 pad_w,
                            u32 depth_mult, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 out_h = nn_out_dim(in_h, kh, stride_h, pad_h)
    u32 out_w = nn_out_dim(in_w, kw, stride_w, pad_w)
    u32 out_c = in_c * depth_mult
    // Strides, once. A depthwise tap walks the input by in_c per kernel column
    // and by in_w*in_c per kernel row, and the 1HWC weights by out_c per column
    // and kw*out_c per row — so every address in the loops below is an add.
    // Unlike conv2d these runs are NOT contiguous (a depthwise output touches
    // one channel, not all of them), so the kj loop stays, but it loses both
    // bounds tests and both multiply chains.
    u32 in_row_bytes = in_w * in_c
    u32 w_row_bytes = kw * out_c

    u32 oy = 0
    while oy < out_h {
        u32 ki_lo = nn_tap_lo(oy, stride_h, pad_h, kh)
        u32 ki_hi = nn_tap_hi(oy, stride_h, pad_h, kh, in_h)
        if ki_hi < ki_lo { ki_hi = ki_lo }
        u32 iy0 = 0
        if ki_hi > ki_lo { iy0 = ((oy * stride_h) + ki_lo) - pad_h }

        u32 ox = 0
        while ox < out_w {
            u32 kj_lo = nn_tap_lo(ox, stride_w, pad_w, kw)
            u32 kj_hi = nn_tap_hi(ox, stride_w, pad_w, kw, in_w)
            if kj_hi < kj_lo { kj_hi = kj_lo }
            u32 ix0 = 0
            if kj_hi > kj_lo { ix0 = ((ox * stride_w) + kj_lo) - pad_w }
            u32 taps = kj_hi - kj_lo
            u32 in_base = in_p + (((iy0 * in_w) + ix0) * in_c)
            u32 w_base = w_p + (ki_lo * w_row_bytes) + (kj_lo * out_c)

            // oc counts up so the output store is sequential; ic/dm are carried
            // alongside instead of being recovered with a divide. oc == ic when
            // depth_mult is 1, which is every depthwise layer in YuNet, but the
            // general mapping oc = ic*depth_mult + dm is preserved exactly.
            u32 oc = 0
            u32 ic = 0
            u32 dm = 0
            while oc < out_c {
                i32 acc = 0
                if bias_p != 0 { acc = nn_load_i32(bias_p + (oc * 4)) }

                u32 ip = in_base + ic
                u32 wp = w_base + oc
                u32 ki = ki_lo
                while ki < ki_hi {
                    acc = nn_dot_i8_str(acc, ip, wp, taps, in_c, out_c, in_zp)
                    ip = ip + in_row_bytes
                    wp = wp + w_row_bytes
                    ki = ki + 1
                }

                u32 qi = (oc * q_stride) * 4
                i32 m = nn_load_i32(mult_p + qi)
                i32 sh = nn_load_i32(shift_p + qi)
                i32 ov = nn_requantize_zp(acc, m, sh, out_zp, act_min, act_max)
                nn_store_i8(out_p + ((((oy * out_w) + ox) * out_c) + oc), ov)
                dm = dm + 1
                if dm == depth_mult {
                    dm = 0
                    ic = ic + 1
                }
                oc = oc + 1
            }
            ox = ox + 1
        }
        oy = oy + 1
    }
}

// 1x1 (pointwise) convolution — stride 1, no padding, output extent equals
// input extent. Weights are [out_c][1][1][in_c], i.e. a plain [out_c][in_c]
// matrix, which is why this is also spelled gemm.
fn nn_pointwise_conv_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c, i32 in_zp,
                          u32 w_p, u32 bias_p, u32 mult_p, u32 shift_p, u32 q_stride,
                          u32 out_p, u32 out_c, i32 out_zp,
                          i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    nn_conv2d_int8(in_p, in_h, in_w, in_c, in_zp,
                   w_p, 1, 1,
                   bias_p, mult_p, shift_p, q_stride,
                   out_p, out_c, out_zp,
                   1, 1, 0, 0,
                   act_min, act_max)
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 4 — dense kernels
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// out[n] = requantize( bias[n] + sum_k (x[k] - x_zp) * W[n][k] )
// W is row-major [n_dim][k_dim] int8 with a zero point of 0 (symmetric).
fn nn_gemv_int8(u32 x_p, u32 k_dim, i32 x_zp,
                u32 w_p, u32 n_dim,
                u32 bias_p, u32 mult_p, u32 shift_p, u32 q_stride,
                u32 out_p, i32 out_zp, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 n = 0
    while n < n_dim {
        i32 acc = 0
        if bias_p != 0 { acc = nn_load_i32(bias_p + (n * 4)) }
        u32 row = w_p + (n * k_dim)
        u32 k = 0
        while k < k_dim {
            i32 xv = nn_load_i8(x_p + k) - x_zp
            i32 wv = nn_load_i8(row + k)
            acc = acc + (xv * wv)
            k = k + 1
        }
        u32 qi = (n * q_stride) * 4
        i32 m = nn_load_i32(mult_p + qi)
        i32 sh = nn_load_i32(shift_p + qi)
        i32 ov = nn_requantize_zp(acc, m, sh, out_zp, act_min, act_max)
        nn_store_i8(out_p + n, ov)
        n = n + 1
    }
}

// out[m][n] = requantize( bias[n] + sum_k (A[m][k] - a_zp) * B[n][k] )
// A is [m_dim][k_dim] int8 activations, B is [n_dim][k_dim] int8 weights.
fn nn_gemm_int8(u32 a_p, u32 m_dim, u32 k_dim, i32 a_zp,
                u32 b_p, u32 n_dim,
                u32 bias_p, u32 mult_p, u32 shift_p, u32 q_stride,
                u32 out_p, i32 out_zp, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 r = 0
    while r < m_dim {
        nn_gemv_int8(a_p + (r * k_dim), k_dim, a_zp,
                     b_p, n_dim,
                     bias_p, mult_p, shift_p, q_stride,
                     out_p + (r * n_dim), out_zp, act_min, act_max)
        r = r + 1
    }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 5 — pooling
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Max pooling over an NHWC int8 tensor. Padded taps are ignored entirely
// (TFLite's behaviour), so a window that is fully outside the input yields
// the int8 minimum clamped into the activation range.
fn nn_maxpool_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c,
                   u32 out_p,
                   u32 kh, u32 kw, u32 stride_h, u32 stride_w,
                   u32 pad_h, u32 pad_w,
                   i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 out_h = nn_out_dim(in_h, kh, stride_h, pad_h)
    u32 out_w = nn_out_dim(in_w, kw, stride_w, pad_w)
    i32 ih_i = in_h
    i32 iw_i = in_w
    i32 pad_h_i = pad_h
    i32 pad_w_i = pad_w

    u32 oy = 0
    while oy < out_h {
        u32 ox = 0
        while ox < out_w {
            u32 c = 0
            while c < in_c {
                i32 best = 0 - 128
                u32 ki = 0
                while ki < kh {
                    u32 iy_base = (oy * stride_h) + ki
                    i32 iy = iy_base
                    iy = iy - pad_h_i
                    if iy >= 0 {
                        if iy < ih_i {
                            u32 iy_u = iy
                            u32 kj = 0
                            while kj < kw {
                                u32 ix_base = (ox * stride_w) + kj
                                i32 ix = ix_base
                                ix = ix - pad_w_i
                                if ix >= 0 {
                                    if ix < iw_i {
                                        u32 ix_u = ix
                                        u32 ipos = (((iy_u * in_w) + ix_u) * in_c) + c
                                        i32 v = nn_load_i8(in_p + ipos)
                                        if v > best { best = v }
                                    }
                                }
                                kj = kj + 1
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    ki = ki + 1
                }
                i32 ov = nn_clamp_i32(best, act_min, act_max)
                nn_store_i8(out_p + ((((oy * out_w) + ox) * in_c) + c), ov)
                c = c + 1
            }
            ox = ox + 1
        }
        oy = oy + 1
    }
}

// Average pooling over an NHWC int8 tensor. Divides by the count of VALID
// (non-padded) taps and rounds half away from zero, matching TFLite's
// `(acc +/- count/2) / count` with C truncating division.
fn nn_avgpool_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c,
                   u32 out_p,
                   u32 kh, u32 kw, u32 stride_h, u32 stride_w,
                   u32 pad_h, u32 pad_w,
                   i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 out_h = nn_out_dim(in_h, kh, stride_h, pad_h)
    u32 out_w = nn_out_dim(in_w, kw, stride_w, pad_w)
    i32 ih_i = in_h
    i32 iw_i = in_w
    i32 pad_h_i = pad_h
    i32 pad_w_i = pad_w

    u32 oy = 0
    while oy < out_h {
        u32 ox = 0
        while ox < out_w {
            u32 c = 0
            while c < in_c {
                i32 acc = 0
                u32 count = 0
                u32 ki = 0
                while ki < kh {
                    u32 iy_base = (oy * stride_h) + ki
                    i32 iy = iy_base
                    iy = iy - pad_h_i
                    if iy >= 0 {
                        if iy < ih_i {
                            u32 iy_u = iy
                            u32 kj = 0
                            while kj < kw {
                                u32 ix_base = (ox * stride_w) + kj
                                i32 ix = ix_base
                                ix = ix - pad_w_i
                                if ix >= 0 {
                                    if ix < iw_i {
                                        u32 ix_u = ix
                                        u32 ipos = (((iy_u * in_w) + ix_u) * in_c) + c
                                        acc = acc + nn_load_i8(in_p + ipos)
                                        count = count + 1
                                    }
                                }
                                kj = kj + 1
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    ki = ki + 1
                }
                i32 ov = 0
                if count != 0 {
                    i32 half = count / 2
                    i32 num = acc
                    if acc > 0 {
                        num = acc + half
                    } else {
                        num = acc - half
                    }
                    ov = nn_sdiv_trunc(num, count)
                }
                ov = nn_clamp_i32(ov, act_min, act_max)
                nn_store_i8(out_p + ((((oy * out_w) + ox) * in_c) + c), ov)
                c = c + 1
            }
            ox = ox + 1
        }
        oy = oy + 1
    }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 6 — activations
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// In-place ReLU in the quantized domain: everything below the zero point
// represents a negative real value, so clamping to zp IS the ReLU.
fn nn_relu_int8(u32 p, u32 n, i32 zp) {
    u32 i = 0
    while i < n {
        i32 v = nn_load_i8(p + i)
        if v < zp { v = zp }
        nn_store_i8(p + i, v)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// In-place ReLU6. `six_q` is the quantized value of the real number 6.0 for
// this tensor, i.e. round(6.0 / scale) + zp — computed offline by the
// quantizer, because there are no floats here. Values above 127 are clipped
// to 127 so the result still fits int8.
fn nn_relu6_int8(u32 p, u32 n, i32 zp, i32 six_q) {
    i32 hi = six_q
    if hi > 127 { hi = 127 }
    u32 i = 0
    while i < n {
        i32 v = nn_load_i8(p + i)
        v = nn_clamp_i32(v, zp, hi)
        nn_store_i8(p + i, v)
        i = i + 1
    }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 7 — elementwise
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Byte copy, used when a layout change has to be materialised rather than
// folded into the tensor's interpretation.
fn nn_copy_i8(u32 dst, u32 src, u32 n) {
    u32 i = 0
    while i < n {
        store8(dst + i, load8(src + i))
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// Quantized elementwise add (the residual connection), TFLite's AddGeneral:
// both inputs are shifted left by `left_shift` (20 in TFLite's own kernels)
// so the two rescalings can be done without losing precision, summed, then
// rescaled once more into the output quantization.
//
//   a_mult/a_shift  rescale input A onto the common intermediate scale
//   b_mult/b_shift  ditto for B
//   o_mult/o_shift  rescale the sum onto the output scale
//
// Zero points are TFLite's raw zero points; they are SUBTRACTED here.
fn nn_add_int8(u32 a_p, i32 a_zp, i32 a_mult, i32 a_shift,
               u32 b_p, i32 b_zp, i32 b_mult, i32 b_shift,
               u32 out_p, i32 out_zp, i32 o_mult, i32 o_shift,
               u32 n, u32 left_shift, i32 act_min, i32 act_max) {
    u32 i = 0
    while i < n {
        i32 av = nn_load_i8(a_p + i) - a_zp
        i32 bv = nn_load_i8(b_p + i) - b_zp
        u32 ua = nn_i32_to_u32(av)
        u32 ub = nn_i32_to_u32(bv)
        ua = (ua << left_shift) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        ub = (ub << left_shift) & 0xFFFFFFFF
        i32 as_v = nn_mul_by_qm(nn_u32_to_i32(ua), a_mult, a_shift)
        i32 bs_v = nn_mul_by_qm(nn_u32_to_i32(ub), b_mult, b_shift)
        i32 sum = as_v + bs_v
        i32 ov = nn_mul_by_qm(sum, o_mult, o_shift) + out_zp
        ov = nn_clamp_i32(ov, act_min, act_max)
        nn_store_i8(out_p + i, ov)
        i = i + 1
    }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 8 — resize
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Nearest-neighbour upsample of an NHWC int8 tensor by INTEGER scale factors.
//
// This is ONNX Resize with mode=nearest, nearest_mode=floor and
// coordinate_transformation_mode=asymmetric, which is the combination every
// FPN-style detector neck uses (YuNet included) and the only one that is a
// pure gather. Asymmetric maps x_in = x_out / scale, and with an integer
// scale the floor is exact, so this is index arithmetic and a byte copy —
// no interpolation, no requantization, and the output inherits the input's
// scale and zero point unchanged.
//
// Non-integer scales are NOT supported: they would need a division per
// output pixel and a rounding rule that ONNX specifies in floating point.
// The caller (the offline converter) rejects them.
fn nn_resize_nearest_int8(u32 in_p, u32 in_h, u32 in_w, u32 in_c,
                          u32 out_p, u32 scale_h, u32 scale_w) {
    if scale_h == 0 { return }
    if scale_w == 0 { return }
    u32 out_h = in_h * scale_h
    u32 out_w = in_w * scale_w
    u32 oy = 0
    while oy < out_h {
        u32 iy = oy / scale_h
        if iy >= in_h { iy = in_h - 1 }
        u32 src_row = in_p + ((iy * in_w) * in_c)
        u32 dst_row = out_p + ((oy * out_w) * in_c)
        u32 ox = 0
        while ox < out_w {
            u32 ix = ox / scale_w
            if ix >= in_w { ix = in_w - 1 }
            u32 s = src_row + (ix * in_c)
            u32 d = dst_row + (ox * in_c)
            u32 c = 0
            while c < in_c {
                store8(d + c, load8(s + c))
                c = c + 1
            }
            ox = ox + 1
        }
        oy = oy + 1
    }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Section 9 — table-driven activations
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Apply a 256-entry int8 -> int8 lookup table elementwise.
//
// WHY A TABLE, and why the decision was not "threshold in logit space":
//
// An int8 tensor has 256 possible values, so ANY elementwise function of it —
// sigmoid, tanh, swish — is fully described by 256 bytes. The table is built
// offline by the converter from the tensor's own input scale/zero-point and
// its output scale/zero-point, so it is not an approximation of the reference
// pipeline, it IS the reference pipeline evaluated at every input it can ever
// see. That makes the on-target result bit-identical to the float-expf host
// simulator by construction, costs one load per element, and puts no expf,
// no floats and no polynomial minimax anywhere near the MCU.
//
// The alternative — never computing sigmoid at all and comparing pre-sigmoid
// logits against logit(threshold), which works because sigmoid is monotonic —
// is cheaper still and is genuinely viable for ranking anchors. It was
// rejected as the default because the model's DECLARED outputs are
// post-sigmoid probabilities: a runner that silently emitted logits would
// produce tensors that do not match the model's own contract, and any
// downstream consumer that mixes cls and obj scores multiplicatively (as
// YuNet's detection post-processing does) needs the actual probabilities,
// not an order-isomorphic surrogate. 256 bytes per Sigmoid node — 1536 bytes
// for the whole of YuNet — is not a price worth arguing about.
//
// Index: an int8 value q maps to table entry q + 128, i.e. -128 -> 0.
fn nn_sigmoid_lut_int8(u32 in_p, u32 n, u32 lut_p, u32 out_p) {
    u32 i = 0
    while i < n {
        u32 v = load8(in_p + i) & 0xFF
        u32 idx = (v + 128) & 0xFF
        store8(out_p + i, load8(lut_p + idx))
        i = i + 1
    }
}
